use std
into sculpt.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with sc_ is exported). It is a port of the storage and compute core of the `Sculpt` class in cad/sculpt.js: the per-vertex and per-triangle arrays, the BVH with its leaf lists and refit, the range, ray and closest-point queries, the corner-table fans and rings, normals, colors, the brush kernels of `one`, and the dynamic topology (`topo` with `splitE`, `collapseE`, `flipE`, `relax`, `compact`) plus `buildO` and `buildCSR`. Results are compared bit for bit with the JavaScript, so every floating-point formula keeps the JavaScript evaluation order exactly: each sum starts at 0.0 and adds its terms one at a time in loop order, products and quotients group left to right as written, and nothing is fused, reordered or vectorized. Reals are f64; an f32 array entry is read as a real and stored rounded to f32 (`as f32`), exactly like a JavaScript Float32Array. Every list keeps JavaScript's element order exactly. Exported functions take and return i32 for integers.

```amni
extern fn sc_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "sc_view_f64";
extern fn sc_view_f32(id i32, xs f32[:]) = "sc_view_f32";
extern fn sc_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "sc_view_i32";
extern fn sc_view_u8(id i32, xs u8[:]) = "sc_view_u8";
extern fn sc_cos(x real) real = "sc_cos";
extern fn sc_sin(x real) real = "sc_sin";
extern fn sc_rand() real = "sc_rand";
extern fn sc_vcp(d i32, s i32) = "sc_vcp";
extern fn sc_vmid(d i32, a i32, b i32) = "sc_vmid";
extern fn sc_fcp(d i32, s i32) = "sc_fcp";
extern fn sc_grow(tri i32, cap i32) = "sc_grow";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `DYN`, `NV`, `NT`, `CAPV`, `CAPT`, `NN`, `CAPN`, `QID`, `DQ`, `AK`, `LFG`, `SID`, `FA`, `OPN`, `HASM`, `HASS`, `SHOW`, `CAV`, `CAT`, `CAF`, `HIDC`, `GALL`, `NF`, `VEP`, `TOP`, `STL`, `RPG`. Reals, all starting at 0.0: `U0`, `U1`, `U2`, `Q0`, `Q1`, `Q2`. Growable arrays: f64 `CO`, `ORIG`, `RS`, `SNP`, `TMP`, `EL`, `SF`; f32 `VN`, `POS`, `COL`, `MASK`, `ON`, `LAY`, `TN`, `NMIN`, `NMAX`, `QD`, `QW`, `CEN`, `SS`; i32 `ST`, `VLEAF`, `VPOS`, `DS`, `NS`, `KS`, `VC`, `TRI`, `IDX`, `TLEAF`, `TPOS`, `TQ`, `OO`, `TSET`, `NL`, `NP`, `LFS`, `RPM`, `LTS`, `LTN`, `LTC`, `LVS`, `LVN`, `LVC`, `LTP`, `LVP`, `QV`, `STK`, `FB`, `NB`, `TB`, `LCV`, `LCT`, `LCF`, `LGV`, `LGT`, `MV`, `LFL`, `CHK`, `FSET`, `T2F`, `NBO`, `NBA`, `VTO`, `VT`, `FOI`, `CVI`, `LEI`, `IDS`, `BS`, `EE`, `QL`, `FL`, `UTL`, `VS`, `AQ`, `RPL`, `RB`, `RD`, `FBA`, `FDA`, `WL`, `SI`; u8 `VH`, `VCHG`, `VGPU`, `VDEAD`, `VLOCK`, `TCHG`, `TGPU`, `TH`, `TDEAD`, `TF`, `FCHG`, `SB`. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array anywhere in this file is done with pi, and every push of a real onto `EL` with pf, never with a bare push; whenever this file says a module array grows by some zeros, or is resized to more entries keeping its entries, that is done with gw.

The per-vertex arrays and their widths are `CO` 3, `VN` 3, `POS` 3, `COL` 3, `MASK` 1, `VH` 1, `ST` 1, `ORIG` 3, `ON` 3, `LAY` 1, `VLEAF` 1, `VPOS` 1, `DS` 1, `NS` 1, `KS` 1, `VCHG` 1, `VGPU` 1, and, only when `DYN` is 1, `VC` 1, `VDEAD` 1, `VLOCK` 1. The per-triangle arrays are `TRI` 3, `IDX` 3, `TN` 3, `TLEAF` 1, `TPOS` 1, `TQ` 1, `TCHG` 1, `TGPU` 1, `TH` 1, and, only when `DYN` is 1, `OO` 3, `TDEAD` 1, `TF` 1, `TSET` 1. A vertex array of width w holds w·`CAPV` entries and a triangle array w·`CAPT` entries; the dynamic-only arrays have length 0 when `DYN` is 0. Every BVH node nd has a triangle list and a vertex list stored in the pools `LTP` and `LVP`: its triangle list is the `LTN`[nd] entries of `LTP` starting at index `LTS`[nd], with room for `LTC`[nd] entries, and `LTC`[nd] equal to −1 means the node has no triangle list; the vertex list is the same with `LVS`, `LVN`, `LVC` and `LVP`. Entry i of a list means the pool entry at the list start plus i. `zr(xs, n)` exists for real, f32, i32 and u8 growable arrays, each a function of three statements: xs is emptied, then xs gets `reserve`(n), then xs is resized to n zeros.

`jmin(a real, b real) real` is JavaScript's Math.min of two numbers: NaN when a or b is NaN; otherwise when a equals b it is a when a is negative zero (1.0 divided by a is below 0) and b otherwise; otherwise the smaller of a and b. `jmax(a real, b real) real` is JavaScript's Math.max of two numbers: NaN when a or b is NaN; otherwise when a equals b it is b when a is negative zero and a otherwise; otherwise the larger of a and b. `hyp3(a real, b real, c real) real` is exactly V8's Math.hypot of three numbers: mx starts at 0.0; for each of a, b and c in order that is not NaN, its absolute value replaces mx when it is above mx; it returns infinity when mx equals infinity, NaN when any of a, b and c is NaN, 0.0 when mx equals 0; otherwise sum and comp start at 0.0 and, for v equal to the absolute values of a, b and c in that order, nv is v divided by mx, su is nv·nv minus comp, pre is sum plus su, comp becomes (pre minus sum) minus su and sum becomes pre; it returns sqrt(sum)·mx. `nx(c int) int` is c minus 2 when c mod 3 equals 2, else c plus 1. `pv(c int) int` is c plus 2 when c mod 3 equals 0, else c minus 1. `nrm(a real, b real, c real)` sets the module reals `U0`, `U1`, `U2` to JavaScript's nrm3 of (a, b, c): l is hyp3(a, b, c), replaced by 1.0 when l is 0 or NaN; `U0` is a divided by l, `U1` is b divided by l and `U2` is c divided by l. `perp(n0 real, n1 real, n2 real)` is JavaScript's perp: e0, e1, e2 are 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 when the absolute value of n0 is below 0.9, else 0.0, 1.0, 0.0; x is n1·e2 minus n2·e1, y is n2·e0 minus n0·e2, z is n0·e1 minus n1·e0; then nrm(x, y, z). `bad(x real) bool` is true when x equals 0 or x is NaN (JavaScript's falsy number).

Growth helpers. `SF`, `SS`, `SI` and `SB` are scratch arrays used only by these helpers. `gw(xs, m)` exists for the same four element types and makes xs hold the larger of its length and m entries, keeping its entries in order and adding zeros: when m is above xs.cap it first moves xs: the scratch of the same element type (`SF` for real, `SS` for f32, `SI` for i32, `SB` for u8) is emptied and then gets `reserve`(the length of xs), then gets a copy of xs with one `append`; xs is emptied and then gets `reserve`((m plus m divided by 2) plus 64), then gets the scratch back with one `append`; the scratch is emptied. Then, when m is above the length of xs, xs is resized to m entries filled with zeros. `cw(xs, m)` exists for i32 and real arrays and makes xs.cap at least m without changing its entries: when m is above xs.cap it moves xs exactly as gw does but with `reserve`(m). `pi(xs, x int)` takes an i32 growable array by reference: when the length of xs is at least xs.cap, cw(xs, 2·(the length of xs) plus 64); then x as i32 is pushed onto xs. `pf(xs, x real)` is the same for a real growable array.

`views()` reports every array and then `VEP` grows by 1: `sc_view_f64` with 1 `CO`, 9 `ORIG`, 50 `RS`; `sc_view_f32` with 2 `VN`, 3 `POS`, 4 `COL`, 5 `MASK`, 10 `ON`, 11 `LAY`, 25 `TN`, 38 `NMIN`, 39 `NMAX`, 44 `QD`, 45 `QW`; `sc_view_i32` with 8 `ST`, 12 `VLEAF`, 13 `VPOS`, 14 `DS`, 15 `NS`, 17 `KS`, 20 `VC`, 23 `TRI`, 24 `IDX`, 27 `TLEAF`, 28 `TPOS`, 29 `TQ`, 33 `OO`, 37 `TSET`, 40 `NL`, 41 `NP`, 42 `LFS`, 43 `QV`, 47 `FSET`, 48 `T2F`, 51 `LCV`, 52 `LCT`, 53 `LCF`, 54 `LGV`, 55 `LGT`, 57 `MV`, 58 `LFL`, 59 `CHK`, 70 `FOI`, 71 `CVI`, 72 `LEI`, 73 `NB`, 74 `FB`, 75 `TB`, 77 `LTS`, 78 `LTN`, 79 `LTC`, 80 `LVS`, 81 `LVN`, 82 `LVC`, 83 `LTP`, 84 `LVP`, 85 `NBO`, 87 `NBA`, 88 `VTO`, 89 `VT`; `sc_view_u8` with 7 `VH`, 18 `VCHG`, 19 `VGPU`, 21 `VDEAD`, 22 `VLOCK`, 30 `TCHG`, 31 `TGPU`, 32 `TH`, 34 `TDEAD`, 35 `TF`, 49 `FCHG`. The exported function `sc_views()` calls views().

The exported function `sc_g(k i32) i32` returns, as i32, `NV` for k 0, `NT` 1, `CAPV` 2, `CAPT` 3, `NN` 4, `CAPN` 5, `QID` 7, `DQ` 8, `AK` 9, `LFG` 10, `SID` 11, `FA` 12, `OPN` 13, `HASM` 14, `HASS` 15, `SHOW` 17, `CAV` 18, `CAT` 19, `CAF` 20, `HIDC` 21, `GALL` 22, `NF` 23, `VEP` 24, the length of `LCV` 25, of `LCT` 27, of `LCF` 28, of `LGV` 29, of `LGT` 30, of `MV` 31, of `LFL` 32, of `CHK` 33, and 0 for any other k. The exported function `sc_s(k i32, x i32)` sets one int to x, using exactly the same key numbers as sc_g: `NV` for k 0, `NT` 1, `CAPV` 2, `CAPT` 3, `NN` 4, `CAPN` 5, `QID` 7, `DQ` 8, `AK` 9, `LFG` 10, `SID` 11, `FA` 12, `OPN` 13, `HASM` 14, `HASS` 15, `SHOW` 17, `CAV` 18, `CAT` 19, `CAF` 20, `HIDC` 21, `GALL` 22, `NF` 23 and `VEP` 24; any other k does nothing.

The exported function `sc_lclear(k i32)` empties (keeping its reserve) `LCV` for k 0, `LCT` 1, `LCF` 2, `LGV` 3, `LGT` 4, `MV` 5, `LFL` 7. The exported function `sc_lpush(k i32, x i32)` pushes x onto the same list with pi.

The exported function `sc_alloc(dyn i32, nv i32, nt i32)` is JavaScript's alloc: `DYN` becomes dyn. When dyn is 1, `CAPV` is ceil((nv as real)·1.3) as an int plus 70 and `CAPT` is ceil((nt as real)·1.3) as an int plus 70; otherwise `CAPV` is the larger of 1 and nv and `CAPT` is the larger of 1 and nt. Every per-vertex array gets zr(it, its width·`CAPV`) and every per-triangle array gets zr(it, its width·`CAPT`), except that when dyn is 0 the dynamic-only arrays are just emptied. Every entry of `VLEAF` and `TLEAF` becomes −1, and every entry of `VC` too when dyn is 1. `NV`, `NT`, `NN`, `CAPN`, `QID`, `DQ`, `AK`, `SID`, `FA`, `OPN`, `HASM`, `HASS`, `SHOW`, `CAV`, `CAT`, `CAF`, `HIDC`, `GALL`, `NF`, `TOP`, `STL` and `RPG` become 0 and `LFG` becomes 1. `QV`, `STK`, `FB`, `NB`, `TB`, `QD` and `QW` are emptied and then resized to 1100 zeros, and `RS` to 16 zeros. `LCV`, `LGV` and `MV` are emptied and then get `reserve`(`CAPV`); `LCT` and `LGT` are emptied and then get `reserve`(`CAPT`); `LCF` and `LFL` are emptied. Then views().

`grow(tri bool, n int)` is JavaScript's grow. cap is `CAPT` when tri is true, else `CAPV`; when n is at most cap, return. c is the larger of n and (ceil((cap as real)·1.5) as an int plus 70). When tri is false: first, when `TOP` is 1 and `STL` is 0, `SNP` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(the length of `CO`), then gets a copy of `CO` with one `append`, and `STL` becomes 1; then each per-vertex array (the dynamic-only ones only when `DYN` is 1) is grown with gw to its width·c entries; the entries of `VLEAF` from index cap below c become −1, and the same entries of `VC` when `DYN` is 1; `CAPV` becomes c; cw(`LCV`, c), cw(`LGV`, c) and cw(`MV`, c). When tri is true: each per-triangle array (the dynamic-only ones only when `DYN` is 1) is grown with gw to its width·c entries; the entries of `TLEAF` from index cap below c become −1; `CAPT` becomes c; cw(`LCT`, c) and cw(`LGT`, c). Last, sc_grow(1 when tri is true else 0, c as i32).

The exported function `sc_static(nf i32)`: `NF` becomes nf; `FSET` and `FCHG` are emptied and then resized to nf zeros and `T2F` to `NT` zeros; `LCF` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(nf); then views(). The exported function `sc_csrin(nfo i32, ncv i32, nle i32)` empties and then resizes `FOI` to nfo zeros, `CVI` to ncv zeros and `LEI` to nle zeros, then views().

The exported function `sc_csr()` is JavaScript's buildCSR with fo = `FOI`, cv = `CVI` and le = `LEI`. cnt is a local i32 list of `NV` plus 1 zeros. A face f below `NF` has a equal to `FOI`[f] and b equal to `FOI`[f+1], and is skipped when b minus a is below 3; for i from a below b, j is i plus 1 when i plus 1 is below b, else a. First pass: for each face that is not skipped and each i from a below b: cnt[`CVI`[i] plus 1] grows by 1, then cnt[`CVI`[j] plus 1] grows by 1. Then for i from 0 while i plus 1 is below the length of `LEI`, stepping by 2: cnt[`LEI`[i] plus 1] grows by 1, then cnt[`LEI`[i+1] plus 1] grows by 1. For v below `NV`, cnt[v+1] grows by cnt[v]. zr(`NBA`, cnt[`NV`]); w is a local copy of the first `NV` entries of cnt; add(u, x) stores x at `NBA`[w[u]] and then w[u] grows by 1. Second pass over the same faces and i: with u equal to `CVI`[i] and x equal to `CVI`[j], add(u, x) and then add(x, u); then for each `LEI` pair i as before, add(`LEI`[i], `LEI`[i+1]) and then add(`LEI`[i+1], `LEI`[i]). `NBO` is emptied and then resized to `NV` plus 1 zeros and m starts at 0; for each v below `NV`: the entries of `NBA` from index cnt[v] below cnt[v+1] are sorted ascending in place; `NBO`[v] becomes m; then for i from cnt[v] below cnt[v+1], when i equals cnt[v] or `NBA`[i] differs from `NBA`[i−1], `NBA`[m] becomes `NBA`[i] and m grows by 1. `NBO`[`NV`] becomes m and `NBA` is resized to m entries. Then `VTO` is emptied and then resized to `NV` plus 1 zeros; for i below 3·`NT`, `VTO`[`TRI`[i] plus 1] grows by 1; for v below `NV`, `VTO`[v+1] grows by `VTO`[v]; `VT` is emptied and then resized to 3·`NT` zeros; q is a local copy of the first `NV` entries of `VTO`; for i below 3·`NT` in order, `VT`[q[`TRI`[i]]] becomes i divided by 3 (integer division) and q[`TRI`[i]] grows by 1. `FOI`, `CVI` and `LEI` are emptied. Then views(). Every local growable array in this paragraph is declared empty and then immediately gets `reserve`(its final length) before it is resized or appended to.

Leaf list helpers. `ltnew(nd int, cnt int)`: `LTS`[nd] becomes the length of `LTP`, `LTN`[nd] and `LTC`[nd] become cnt, and gw(`LTP`, its length plus cnt) adds cnt zeros (the caller fills them). `lvnew(nd int)`: `LVS`[nd] becomes the length of `LVP` and `LVN`[nd] and `LVC`[nd] become 0. `ltpush(nd int, x int)`: when `LTN`[nd] equals `LTC`[nd]: nc is the larger of 8 and 2·`LTC`[nd]; when `LTS`[nd] plus `LTC`[nd] equals the length of `LTP`, gw(`LTP`, its length plus (nc minus `LTC`[nd])); otherwise s is the length of `LTP`, gw(`LTP`, s plus nc), the `LTN`[nd] entries of `LTP` starting at `LTS`[nd] are copied in order to index s onward, and `LTS`[nd] becomes s; then `LTC`[nd] becomes nc. Then `LTP`[`LTS`[nd] plus `LTN`[nd]] becomes x and `LTN`[nd] grows by 1. `lvpush(nd int, x int)` is the same with `LVS`, `LVN`, `LVC` and `LVP`.

`node(p int) int` is JavaScript's node: n is `NN` and `NN` grows by 1. When n is at least `CAPN`: c is ceil((`CAPN` as real)·1.5) as an int plus 70; `NMIN` and `NMAX` are grown with gw to 3·c entries and `NL`, `NP`, `LFS`, `RPM`, `LTS`, `LTN`, `LTC`, `LVS`, `LVN` and `LVC` with gw to c entries; `CAPN` becomes c. Then `NP`[n] becomes p, `NL`[n] becomes −1, `LFS`[n] becomes 0, `LTC`[n] and `LVC`[n] become −1, `LTN`[n] and `LVN`[n] become 0, and it returns n.

`leafB(nd int)`: lo0, lo1, lo2 start at 1e30 and hi0, hi1, hi2 at −1e30; ad(v) means: for each axis k below 3, with x equal to `CO`[3v+k], when x is below lo of k, lo of k becomes x; when x is above hi of k, hi of k becomes x. For each entry t of the triangle list of nd in order: ad(`TRI`[3t]), ad(`TRI`[3t+1]), ad(`TRI`[3t+2]). Then for each entry v of the vertex list of nd in order, ad(v). Then for k below 3, `NMIN`[3nd+k] becomes lo of k as f32 and `NMAX`[3nd+k] becomes hi of k as f32. `nodeB(nd int)`: l is 3·`NL`[nd], r is l plus 3 and o is 3nd; for k below 3, `NMIN`[o+k] becomes jmin(`NMIN`[l+k], `NMIN`[r+k]) as f32 and `NMAX`[o+k] becomes jmax(`NMAX`[l+k], `NMAX`[r+k]) as f32. `lfMark(nd int)`: when nd is at least 0 and `LFS`[nd] differs from `LFG`, `LFS`[nd] becomes `LFG` and nd is pushed onto `LFL`. `ownV(v int, lf int)`: `VLEAF`[v] becomes lf, `VPOS`[v] becomes `LVN`[lf], lvpush(lf, v), then lfMark(lf). `rmT(t int)`: lf is `TLEAF`[t]; return when lf is below 0; i is `TPOS`[t]; `LTN`[lf] decreases by 1 and last is entry `LTN`[lf] of the triangle list of lf; when last differs from t, entry i of that list becomes last and `TPOS`[last] becomes i; `TLEAF`[t] becomes −1; lfMark(lf). `rmV(v int)` is the same with `VLEAF`, `VPOS` and the vertex list of lf. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

The exported function `sc_bvh()` is JavaScript's bvh. `CEN` is emptied and then resized to 3·(the larger of 1 and `NT`) zeros and `IDS` to `NT` zeros; m starts at 0; for each t below `NT`, skipping t when `DYN` is 1 and `TDEAD`[t] is not 0: `IDS`[m] becomes t and m grows by 1; a, b and c are 3·`TRI`[3t], 3·`TRI`[3t+1] and 3·`TRI`[3t+2]; for k below 3, `CEN`[3t+k] becomes (((`CO`[a+k] plus `CO`[b+k]) plus `CO`[c+k]) divided by 3.0) as f32. `CAPN` becomes the larger of 7 and 4·((m plus 511) divided by 512, integer division) plus 7; `NN` becomes 0; `NMIN` and `NMAX` are emptied and then resized to 3·`CAPN` zeros; `NL`, `NP`, `LFS`, `RPM`, `LTS`, `LTN`, `LTC`, `LVS`, `LVN` and `LVC` are emptied and then resized to `CAPN` zeros; `LTP` and `LVP` are emptied; every entry of `VLEAF` becomes −1. The key of a triangle t on axis ax is `CEN`[3t+ax] read as a real. sel(lo, hi, k, ax) is the quickselect on `IDS`: hi decreases by 1; while hi is above lo: p is the key of `IDS`[(lo plus hi) divided by 2, integer division]; i is lo and j is hi; while i is at most j: while the key of `IDS`[i] is below p, i grows by 1; while the key of `IDS`[j] is above p, j decreases by 1; when i is at most j, `IDS`[i] and `IDS`[j] are swapped, i grows by 1 and j decreases by 1. Then when k is at most j, hi becomes j; otherwise when k is at least i, lo becomes i; otherwise the while loop stops. The build uses `BS` as a stack of triples (nd, s, e): it is emptied and then holds (node(−1), 0, m). While `BS` is not empty, the last triple (nd, s, e) is popped. When e minus s is at most 512: ltnew(nd, e minus s) and entry i of its triangle list becomes `IDS`[s+i] for i below e minus s; for each entry t at position i of that list, `TLEAF`[t] becomes nd and `TPOS`[t] becomes i; lvnew(nd); and the loop continues. Otherwise lo of k starts at 1e30 and hi of k at −1e30 for k below 3; for i from s below e and k below 3, with x the key of `IDS`[i] on axis k, when x is below lo of k it replaces it and when x is above hi of k it replaces it. ex of k is hi of k minus lo of k; ax is 0 when ex0 is at least ex1 and ex0 is at least ex2, else 1 when ex1 is at least ex2, else 2; k is (s plus e) divided by 2 (integer division); sel(s, e, k, ax); l is node(nd); node(nd) is called once more; `NL`[nd] becomes l; the triple (l, s, k) is pushed and then (l plus 1, k, e). After the build, for each nd below `NN` in order with `NL`[nd] below 0, for each entry t of its triangle list in order and each k below 3: v is `TRI`[3t+k], and when `VLEAF`[v] is below 0, ownV(v, nd). Then for nd from `NN` minus 1 down to 0: leafB(nd) when `NL`[nd] is below 0, else nodeB(nd). `LFL` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(`CAPN`). Then views(). Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

`splitLeaf(nd int)` is JavaScript's splitLeaf. n is `LTN`[nd] and s is `LTS`[nd]. key(t, k) is (`CO`[3·`TRI`[3t]+k] plus `CO`[3·`TRI`[3t+1]+k]) plus `CO`[3·`TRI`[3t+2]+k]. lo of k starts at 1e30 and hi of k at −1e30; for each entry t of the triangle list in order and k below 3, with x equal to key(t, k), when x is below lo of k it replaces it and when x is above hi of k it replaces it. ex and ax as in sc_bvh. The n entries of `LTP` from index s are stable-sorted in place by key(t, ax) ascending (an entry goes before another only when its key is smaller). h is n divided by 2 (integer division); l is node(nd); r is node(nd); `NL`[nd] becomes l. ltnew(l, h) and entry i of the list of l becomes `LTP`[s+i] for i below h; ltnew(r, n minus h) and entry i of the list of r becomes `LTP`[s+h+i] for i below n minus h; lvnew(l); lvnew(r). For q equal to l and then r: for each entry t at position i of the triangle list of q, `TLEAF`[t] becomes q and `TPOS`[t] becomes i; then leafB(q). mid is (`NMAX`[3l+ax] plus `NMIN`[3r+ax]) divided by 2.0. `WL` is emptied and then gets the `LVN`[nd] entries of the vertex list of nd in order; `LTC`[nd] and `LVC`[nd] become −1 and `LTN`[nd] and `LVN`[nd] become 0. For each v of `WL` in order: ownV(v, l) when `CO`[3v+ax] is at most mid, else ownV(v, r). Then leafB(l), leafB(r), and `LFS`[l] and `LFS`[r] become `LFG`. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

The exported function `sc_refit()` is JavaScript's refit, followed by views() on every path. When `LFL` is empty, return. L is a local copy of `LFL`; `LFL` is emptied (keeping its reserve); `LFG` grows by 1; `RPG` grows by 1; `RPL` is emptied. up(nd): p is nd; while p is at least 0 and `RPM`[p] differs from `RPG`: `RPM`[p] becomes `RPG`, p is pushed onto `RPL` and p becomes `NP`[p]. For each nd of L in order: skip it when `NL`[nd] is at least 0; when `DYN` is 1 and `LTN`[nd] is above 2048: splitLeaf(nd), up(nd), and go on to the next nd; otherwise leafB(nd) and up(`NP`[nd]). Then `RPL` is sorted descending and for each p of `RPL` in that order, nodeB(p) when `NL`[p] is at least 0. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf. Every local growable array in this paragraph is declared empty and then immediately gets `reserve`(its final length) before it is resized or appended to.

`qgrow()`: n is 2 times the length of `QV`; `QV`, `QD` and `QW` are grown with gw to n entries. `range(x real, y real, z real, r real) int` is JavaScript's range: r2 is r·r; `STK`[0] becomes 0, sp is 1 and n is 0. While sp is above 0: sp decreases by 1 and nd is `STK`[sp]; o is 3nd; d starts at 0.0; for the axes in order 0, 1, 2 with p equal to x, y and z: q is `NMIN`[o+k] minus p when p is below `NMIN`[o+k], else p minus `NMAX`[o+k] when p is above `NMAX`[o+k], else 0.0, and d grows by q·q. When d is above r2, continue. l is `NL`[nd]; when l is at least 0: `STK`[sp] becomes l, `STK`[sp+1] becomes l plus 1, sp grows by 2, continue. For each v of the vertex list of nd in order: skip v when `VH`[v] is not 0; a is 3v; dx is `CO`[a] minus x, dy is `CO`[a+1] minus y, dz is `CO`[a+2] minus z; e is (dx·dx plus dy·dy) plus dz·dz; when e is at most r2: qgrow() when n is at least the length of `QV`; `QV`[n] becomes v, `QD`[n] becomes sqrt(e) as f32 and n grows by 1. It returns n. The exported function `sc_range(x real, y real, z real, r real) i32` computes n as range(x, y, z, r), calls views() and returns n as i32.

The exported function `sc_ray(o0 real, o1 real, o2 real, d0 real, d1 real, d2 real, tmax real) i32` is JavaScript's ray. ix is 1.0 divided by d0, iy is 1.0 divided by d1, iz is 1.0 divided by d2; bt is tmax, bi is −1, bu and bw are 0.0. `STK`[0] becomes 0 and sp is 1. While sp is above 0: sp decreases by 1, nd is `STK`[sp] and q is 3nd; t0 is (`NMIN`[q] minus o0)·ix and t1 is (`NMAX`[q] minus o0)·ix, and when t0 is above t1 they are swapped; a is (`NMIN`[q+1] minus o1)·iy and b is (`NMAX`[q+1] minus o1)·iy, swapped when a is above b; t0 becomes jmax(t0, a) and t1 becomes jmin(t1, b); a is (`NMIN`[q+2] minus o2)·iz and b is (`NMAX`[q+2] minus o2)·iz, swapped when a is above b; t0 becomes jmax(t0, a) and t1 becomes jmin(t1, b). When t1 is below jmax(t0, 0.0) or t0 is above bt, continue. l is `NL`[nd]; when l is at least 0, push l and then l plus 1 onto `STK` as in range and continue. For each t of the triangle list of nd in order, skipping t when `TH`[t] is not 0: A, B and E are 3·`TRI`[3t], 3·`TRI`[3t+1] and 3·`TRI`[3t+2]; ex is `CO`[B] minus `CO`[A], ey is `CO`[B+1] minus `CO`[A+1], ez is `CO`[B+2] minus `CO`[A+2]; fx is `CO`[E] minus `CO`[A], fy is `CO`[E+1] minus `CO`[A+1], fz is `CO`[E+2] minus `CO`[A+2]; px is d1·fz minus d2·fy, py is d2·fx minus d0·fz, pz is d0·fy minus d1·fx; det is (ex·px plus ey·py) plus ez·pz; skip t when the absolute value of det is below 1e-30; id is 1.0 divided by det; sx is o0 minus `CO`[A], sy is o1 minus `CO`[A+1], sz is o2 minus `CO`[A+2]; u is ((sx·px plus sy·py) plus sz·pz)·id; skip t when u is below 0 or above 1; qx is sy·ez minus sz·ey, qy is sz·ex minus sx·ez, qz is sx·ey minus sy·ex; v is ((d0·qx plus d1·qy) plus d2·qz)·id; skip t when v is below 0 or u plus v is above 1; tt is ((fx·qx plus fy·qy) plus fz·qz)·id; when tt is above 0 and tt is below bt: bt becomes tt, bi becomes t, bu becomes u and bw becomes v. After the loop it returns 0 when bi is below 0. Otherwise a, b and c are 3·`TRI`[3bi], 3·`TRI`[3bi+1] and 3·`TRI`[3bi+2]; w0 is (1.0 minus bu) minus bw; nrm((`VN`[a]·w0 plus `VN`[b]·bu) plus `VN`[c]·bw, (`VN`[a+1]·w0 plus `VN`[b+1]·bu) plus `VN`[c+1]·bw, (`VN`[a+2]·w0 plus `VN`[b+2]·bu) plus `VN`[c+2]·bw); `RS`[0] becomes bt, `RS`[1] becomes bi as a real, `RS`[2] becomes o0 plus d0·bt, `RS`[3] becomes o1 plus d1·bt, `RS`[4] becomes o2 plus d2·bt, `RS`[5], `RS`[6] and `RS`[7] become `U0`, `U1` and `U2`; it returns 1.

`cpt(p0 real, p1 real, p2 real, a int, b int, c int)` is JavaScript's cpTri with C equal to `CO` and sets the module reals `Q0`, `Q1`, `Q2` to the closest point. With k standing for 0, 1 and 2 (each written out): Ak is `CO`[a+k], Bk is `CO`[b+k], Ck is `CO`[c+k]; abk is Bk minus Ak, ack is Ck minus Ak, apk is pk minus Ak. dot(x, y) means (x0·y0 plus x1·y1) plus x2·y2. d1 is dot(ab, ap) and d2 is dot(ac, ap); when d1 is at most 0 and d2 is at most 0, Q is A and return. bpk is pk minus Bk; d3 is dot(ab, bp) and d4 is dot(ac, bp); when d3 is at least 0 and d4 is at most d3, Q is B and return. vc is d1·d4 minus d3·d2; when vc is at most 0, d1 is at least 0 and d3 is at most 0: v is d1 divided by (d1 minus d3), Qk is Ak plus abk·v, return. cpk is pk minus Ck; d5 is dot(ab, cp) and d7 is dot(ac, cp); when d7 is at least 0 and d5 is at most d7, Q is C and return. vb is d5·d2 minus d1·d7; when vb is at most 0, d2 is at least 0 and d7 is at most 0: w is d2 divided by (d2 minus d7), Qk is Ak plus ack·w, return. va is d3·d7 minus d5·d4; when va is at most 0, d4 minus d3 is at least 0 and d5 minus d7 is at least 0: w is (d4 minus d3) divided by ((d4 minus d3) plus (d5 minus d7)), Qk is Bk plus (Ck minus Bk)·w, return. Otherwise dn is 1.0 divided by ((va plus vb) plus vc), v is vb·dn, w is vc·dn and Qk is (Ak plus abk·v) plus ack·w.

The exported function `sc_closest(p0 real, p1 real, p2 real) i32` is JavaScript's closest. bd is infinity, found is false and bt is −1; b0, b1, b2 are 0.0. `STK`[0] becomes 0 and sp is 1. While sp is above 0: sp decreases by 1, nd is `STK`[sp], o is 3nd and d starts at 0.0; for k equal to 0, 1 and 2 in order with pk the matching p0, p1 or p2: q is `NMIN`[o+k] minus pk when pk is below `NMIN`[o+k], else pk minus `NMAX`[o+k] when pk is above `NMAX`[o+k], else 0.0, and d grows by q·q. When d is at least bd, continue. When `NL`[nd] is at least 0 push its two children as in range and continue. For each t of the triangle list of nd in order, skipping t when `TH`[t] is not 0: cpt(p0, p1, p2, 3·`TRI`[3t], 3·`TRI`[3t+1], 3·`TRI`[3t+2]); e is ((`Q0` minus p0)·(`Q0` minus p0) plus (`Q1` minus p1)·(`Q1` minus p1)) plus (`Q2` minus p2)·(`Q2` minus p2); when e is below bd: bd becomes e, found becomes true, b0, b1, b2 become `Q0`, `Q1`, `Q2` and bt becomes t. It returns 0 when found is false. Otherwise nrm(`TN`[3bt], `TN`[3bt+1], `TN`[3bt+2]); `RS`[0], `RS`[1], `RS`[2] become b0, b1, b2, `RS`[3] becomes bt as a real, `RS`[4] becomes sqrt(bd), `RS`[5], `RS`[6], `RS`[7] become `U0`, `U1`, `U2`; it returns 1.

`fan(v int) int` is JavaScript's fan writing into `FB`: c0 is `VC`[v]; `FA` becomes 0 and `OPN` becomes 0; it returns 0 when c0 is below 0. n is 0 and c is c0; repeat: `FB`[n] becomes c and n grows by 1; p is `OO`[pv(c)]; c becomes −1 when p is below 0, else pv(p); and repeat again only while c is at least 0, c differs from c0 and n is below 1000. `FA` becomes n. When c is below 0: `OPN` becomes 1; p is `OO`[nx(c0)]; c becomes −1 when p is below 0, else nx(p); while c is at least 0 and n is below 1000: `FB`[n] becomes c, n grows by 1, r is `OO`[nx(c)] and c becomes −1 when r is below 0, else nx(r). It returns n. `nbr(v int) int` is JavaScript's nbr writing into `NB`: when `DYN` is 0: s is `NBO`[v] and e is `NBO`[v+1]; for i from s below e, `NB`[i−s] becomes `NBA`[i] when i minus s is below 1100; it returns e minus s. Otherwise n is fan(v) and m is 0; for i below `FA`: `NB`[m] becomes `TRI`[nx(`FB`[i])] and m grows by 1; when `OPN` is 1 and n is above 0: `NB`[m] becomes `TRI`[pv(`FB`[0])] and m grows by 1, then for i from `FA` below n, `NB`[m] becomes `TRI`[pv(`FB`[i])] and m grows by 1. It returns m. `vtris(v int) int` is JavaScript's vtris writing into `TB`: when `DYN` is 0: s is `VTO`[v] and e is `VTO`[v+1]; for i from s below e, `TB`[i−s] becomes `VT`[i] when i minus s is below 1100; it returns e minus s. Otherwise n is fan(v), `TB`[i] becomes `FB`[i] divided by 3 (integer division) for i below n, and it returns n. The exported functions `sc_fan(v i32) i32`, `sc_nbr(v i32) i32` and `sc_vtris(v i32) i32` return fan(v), nbr(v) and vtris(v) as i32.

`chgV(v int)`: when `VCHG`[v] is 0, `VCHG`[v] becomes 1 and v is pushed onto `LCV`; then gpuV(v). `gpuV(v int)`: when `VGPU`[v] is 0, `VGPU`[v] becomes 1 and v is pushed onto `LGV`. `chgT(t int)`: when `TCHG`[t] is 0, `TCHG`[t] becomes 1 and t is pushed onto `LCT`; then gpuT(t). `gpuT(t int)`: when `TGPU`[t] is 0, `TGPU`[t] becomes 1 and t is pushed onto `LGT`. `chgF(f int)`: when `FCHG`[f] is 0, `FCHG`[f] becomes 1 and f is pushed onto `LCF`. The exported function `sc_clearChg()` is JavaScript's clearChg: for each v of `LCV` that is below `CAPV`, `VCHG`[v] becomes 0; for each t of `LCT` that is below `CAPT`, `TCHG`[t] becomes 0; for each f of `LCF`, `FCHG`[f] becomes 0; `LCV`, `LCT` and `LCF` are emptied (keeping their reserves); `CAV`, `CAT`, `CAF` and `HIDC` become 0; then views(). Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

`addV(src int) int` is JavaScript's addV: v is `NV` and `NV` grows by 1; grow(false, `NV`); a is 3v and b is 3src; for k below 3: `CO`[a+k] becomes `CO`[b+k], `POS`[a+k] becomes `POS`[b+k], `VN`[a+k] becomes `VN`[b+k] and `COL`[a+k] becomes `COL`[b+k]; `MASK`[v] becomes `MASK`[src]; sc_vcp(v as i32, src as i32); `VLEAF`[v] becomes −1; `VDEAD`[v], `VLOCK`[v] and `VH`[v] become 0; `VC`[v] becomes −1; `ST`[v] and `LAY`[v] become 0; chgV(v); it returns v. `addT(src int) int` is JavaScript's addT: t is `NT` and `NT` grows by 1; grow(true, `NT`); `TF`[t] becomes `TF`[src], `TSET`[t] becomes `TSET`[src], `TH`[t] becomes `TH`[src] and `TDEAD`[t] becomes 0; sc_fcp(t as i32, src as i32); lf is `TLEAF`[src]; `TLEAF`[t] becomes lf; `TPOS`[t] becomes `LTN`[lf]; ltpush(lf, t); lfMark(lf); chgT(t); it returns t.

`hidAll()` is JavaScript's hidAll: `VH`[v] becomes 1 for v below `NV`; then for each t below `NT`, skipping t when `TH`[t] is not 0 or (`DYN` is 1 and `TDEAD`[t] is not 0): `VH`[`TRI`[3t]], `VH`[`TRI`[3t+1]] and `VH`[`TRI`[3t+2]] become 0. `idxT(t int)`: o is 3t; when `TH`[t] is not 0 or (`DYN` is 1 and `TDEAD`[t] is not 0), `IDX`[o], `IDX`[o+1] and `IDX`[o+2] all become `TRI`[o]; otherwise `IDX`[o+k] becomes `TRI`[o+k] for k below 3. The exported function `sc_hidAll()` calls hidAll() and then views(). The exported function `sc_idxAll()` calls idxT(t) for each t below `NT` and then views(). The exported function `sc_flush0()`: idxT(t) for each t of `LGT` in order; when `GALL` is not 0: idxT(t) for each t below `NT`, `VGPU`[v] becomes 0 for each v of `LGV` and `TGPU`[t] becomes 0 for each t of `LGT`; then views().

`triN(t int)` is JavaScript's triN: a, b and c are 3·`TRI`[3t], 3·`TRI`[3t+1] and 3·`TRI`[3t+2]; ux is `CO`[b] minus `CO`[a], uy is `CO`[b+1] minus `CO`[a+1], uz is `CO`[b+2] minus `CO`[a+2]; wx is `CO`[c] minus `CO`[a], wy is `CO`[c+1] minus `CO`[a+1], wz is `CO`[c+2] minus `CO`[a+2]; o is 3t; `TN`[o] becomes (uy·wz minus uz·wy) as f32, `TN`[o+1] becomes (uz·wx minus ux·wz) as f32 and `TN`[o+2] becomes (ux·wy minus uy·wx) as f32. `vertN(v int)` is JavaScript's vertN: n is vtris(v); x, y and z start at 0.0; for i below n, with o equal to 3·`TB`[i]: x grows by `TN`[o], y by `TN`[o+1] and z by `TN`[o+2]; l is hyp3(x, y, z), replaced by 1.0 when it is 0 or NaN; a is 3v; `VN`[a] becomes (x divided by l) as f32, `VN`[a+1] becomes (y divided by l) as f32 and `VN`[a+2] becomes (z divided by l) as f32.

The exported function `sc_normAll()` is JavaScript's normAll: `VN`[i] becomes 0 for i below 3·`NV`. For each t below `NT`, skipping t when `DYN` is 1 and `TDEAD`[t] is not 0: triN(t); o is 3t; for k below 3 in order, with a equal to 3·`TRI`[o+k]: `VN`[a] becomes (`VN`[a] plus `TN`[o]) as f32, `VN`[a+1] becomes (`VN`[a+1] plus `TN`[o+1]) as f32 and `VN`[a+2] becomes (`VN`[a+2] plus `TN`[o+2]) as f32. Then for each v below `NV`: a is 3v; l is hyp3(`VN`[a], `VN`[a+1], `VN`[a+2]), replaced by 1.0 when it is 0 or NaN; `VN`[a] becomes (`VN`[a] divided by l) as f32, then `VN`[a+1] becomes (`VN`[a+1] divided by l) as f32, then `VN`[a+2] becomes (`VN`[a+2] divided by l) as f32. Then views().

The exported function `sc_updN()` is JavaScript's updN over `MV`: `QID` grows by 1 and s is `QID`; `UTL` is emptied. For i below the length of `MV`: v is `MV`[i]; skip v when `DYN` is 1 and `VDEAD`[v] is not 0; when `VLEAF`[v] is at least 0, lfMark(`VLEAF`[v]); m is vtris(v); for j below m: t is `TB`[j]; when `TQ`[t] differs from s: `TQ`[t] becomes s, triN(t), t is pushed onto `UTL`, lfMark(`TLEAF`[t]). Then for each t of `UTL` in order and each k below 3: v is `TRI`[3t+k]; when `NS`[v] differs from s: `NS`[v] becomes s, vertN(v), gpuV(v). Then views(). Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

`setOf(v int) int`: when `DYN` is 1: c is `VC`[v] and it returns 0 when c is below 0, else `TSET`[c divided by 3, integer division]. Otherwise i is `VTO`[v] and it returns `FSET`[`T2F`[`VT`[i]]] when i is below `VTO`[v+1], else 0. `colV(v int)` is JavaScript's colV: m is 1.0 minus 0.7·jmin(1.0, jmax(0.0, `MASK`[v])); s is setOf(v) when `SHOW` is not 0, else 0; a is 3v. When s is not 0: h is (s·2654435761) bitwise-and 4294967295 computed in 64-bit ints (JavaScript's Math.imul(s, 2654435761) unsigned); `COL`[a] becomes (m·(0.45 plus 0.55·(((h bitwise-and 255) as real) divided by 255.0))) as f32, `COL`[a+1] the same with h shifted right by 8 bits, and `COL`[a+2] the same with h shifted right by 16 bits. Otherwise `COL`[a], `COL`[a+1] and `COL`[a+2] all become m as f32. The exported function `sc_colAll()` calls colV(v) for each v below `NV`, sets `GALL` to 1 and calls views(). The exported function `sc_colv(v i32)` calls colV(v). `maskV(v int)`: `HASM` becomes 1, chgV(v), colV(v). `setFS(t int, id int)` is JavaScript's setFS: `HASS` becomes 1; when `DYN` is 1: return when `TSET`[t] equals id; `TSET`[t] becomes id; chgT(t). Otherwise f is `T2F`[t]; return when `FSET`[f] equals id; `FSET`[f] becomes id; chgF(f). Then when `SHOW` is not 0, for k below 3: v is `TRI`[3t+k], colV(v), gpuV(v).

`cap(v int)`: when `ST`[v] differs from `SID`: `ST`[v] becomes `SID`; a is 3v; for k below 3, `ORIG`[a+k] becomes `CO`[a+k] and `ON`[a+k] becomes `VN`[a+k]; `LAY`[v] becomes 0. `set(v int, x real, y real, z real)`: a is 3v; `CO`[a], `CO`[a+1], `CO`[a+2] become x, y, z; `POS`[a], `POS`[a+1], `POS`[a+2] become x, y, z as f32; chgV(v); when `DS`[v] differs from `DQ`, `DS`[v] becomes `DQ` and v is pushed onto `MV`. The exported functions `sc_cap(v i32)` and `sc_set(v i32, x real, y real, z real)` call cap(v) and set(v, x, y, z). The exported function `sc_mv0()` empties `MV` (keeping its reserve) and `DQ` grows by 1. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

The exported function `sc_bbox() i32` is JavaScript's bbox: lo0, lo1, lo2 start at 1e30 and hi0, hi1, hi2 at −1e30; for each v below `NV`, skipping v when `DYN` is 1 and `VDEAD`[v] is not 0, for k below 3, with x equal to `CO`[3v+k]: when x is below lo of k it replaces it; when x is above hi of k it replaces it. `RS`[0], `RS`[1], `RS`[2] become lo0, lo1, lo2 and `RS`[3], `RS`[4], `RS`[5] become hi0, hi1, hi2. It returns 1 when lo0 is above hi0, else 0.

`autoMask(n int) int` is JavaScript's autoMask: s1 is `AK` plus 1 and s2 is `AK` plus 2, and `AK` grows by 2; b is 0; for i below n: `KS`[`QV`[i]] becomes s1, and when `QD`[i] is below `QD`[b], b becomes i. `AQ` is emptied and `QV`[b] is pushed onto it; `KS`[`QV`[b]] becomes s2; for h from 0 while h is below the length of `AQ` (which grows meanwhile): m is nbr(`AQ`[h]); for j below m: w is `NB`[j]; when `KS`[w] equals s1, `KS`[w] becomes s2 and w is pushed onto `AQ`. Then m is 0; for i below n, when `KS`[`QV`[i]] equals s2: `QV`[m] becomes `QV`[i], `QD`[m] becomes `QD`[i] and m grows by 1. It returns m. `pick(c0 real, c1 real, c2 real, r real, w0 real, w1 real, w2 real, front int, auto int) int` is JavaScript's pick: n is range(c0, c1, c2, r); when front is not 0: m is 0; for i below n: a is 3·`QV`[i]; when (`VN`[a]·w0 plus `VN`[a+1]·w1) plus `VN`[a+2]·w2 is below 0.05: `QV`[m] becomes `QV`[i], `QD`[m] becomes `QD`[i] and m grows by 1; n becomes m. It returns autoMask(n) when auto is not 0 and n is above 0, else n. The exported function `sc_pick(c0 real, c1 real, c2 real, r real, w0 real, w1 real, w2 real, front i32, auto i32) i32` computes n as pick(…), calls views() and returns n as i32. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

`fall(code int, x real) real` is the brush falloff: for code 1, sqrt(jmax(0.0, 2.0·x minus x·x)); for 2, sqrt(x); for 3, x·(2.0 minus x); for 4, x·x; for 5, x; for 7, 1.0; for 8, x·sc_rand(); for 9, ((x·x)·x)·(x·(x·6.0 minus 15.0) plus 10.0); for 0 and any other code, (x·x)·(3.0 minus 2.0·x).

The exported function `sc_one(tool i32, c0 real, c1 real, c2 real, R real, S real, w0 real, w1 real, w2 real, hd i32, d0 real, d1 real, d2 real, hl i32, l0 real, l1 real, l2 real, fc i32, inv i32, sf real, ang real, front i32, auto i32, fsid i32)` is the first half of JavaScript's one(tool, c, R, S, vw, dr, dl, st), with c = (c0, c1, c2), vw = (w0, w1, w2), the direction dr = (d0, d1, d2) present only when hd is 1, the drag delta dl = (l0, l1, l2) present only when hl is 1, falloff fall(fc, ·) and fsid as st.fsId; it calls views() before every return. sq is true when tool is 8. n is pick(c0, c1, c2, R·1.4142135623730951 when sq is true else R, w0, w1, w2, front, auto); when n is 0, views() and return. The normal array NA means `ON` when tool is 1, else `VN`. sg is −1.0 when inv is not 0, else 1.0; mk is true when tool is 19 or 20. ax, ay, az, cx, cy, cz and sw start at 0.0. For i below n in order: v is `QV`[i]; a is 3v; f is fall(fc, jmax(0.0, 1.0 minus `QD`[i] divided by R)); cap(v); `QW`[i] becomes (f·(1.0 when mk is true, else 1.0 minus `MASK`[v])) as f32; ax grows by NA[a]·f, ay by NA[a+1]·f, az by NA[a+2]·f, cx by `CO`[a]·f, cy by `CO`[a+1]·f, cz by `CO`[a+2]·f and sw by f. al is hyp3(ax, ay, az); when al is above 1e-12, na0, na1, na2 are ax divided by al, ay divided by al, az divided by al; otherwise nrm(−w0, −w1, −w2) and na0, na1, na2 are `U0`, `U1`, `U2`. When sw is above 1e-12, ca0, ca1, ca2 are cx divided by sw, cy divided by sw, cz divided by sw; otherwise they are c0, c1, c2. t0, t1, t2, b0, b1, b2 start at 0.0. When sq is true or tool is 12: when hd is 1 and hyp3(d0, d1, d2) is above 1e-12, e0, e1, e2 are d0, d1, d2; otherwise perp(na0, na1, na2) and e0, e1, e2 are `U0`, `U1`, `U2`; e is (e0·na0 plus e1·na1) plus e2·na2; t0 is e0 minus na0·e, t1 is e1 minus na1·e, t2 is e2 minus na2·e; when hyp3(t0, t1, t2) is above 1e-12, nrm(t0, t1, t2), else perp(na0, na1, na2), and t0, t1, t2 become `U0`, `U1`, `U2`; b0 is na1·t2 minus na2·t1, b1 is na2·t0 minus na0·t2, b2 is na0·t1 minus na1·t0. When sq is true: for i below n in order: v is `QV`[i]; a is 3v; x is ((`CO`[a] minus c0)·t0 plus (`CO`[a+1] minus c1)·t1) plus (`CO`[a+2] minus c2)·t2; y is ((`CO`[a] minus c0)·b0 plus (`CO`[a+1] minus c1)·b1) plus (`CO`[a+2] minus c2)·b2; q is (jmax(absolute value of x, absolute value of y)·1.4142135623730951) divided by R; `QW`[i] becomes 0 when q is at least 1, else (fall(fc, 1.0 minus q)·(1.0 minus `MASK`[v])) as f32. k is (((R·S)·0.5)·sf)·sg. Then it calls tools(tool, n, c0, c1, c2, R, S, na0, na1, na2, ca0, ca1, ca2, b0, b1, b2, hl, l0, l1, l2, sg, k, ang, fsid), then views().

`tools(tool int, n int, c0 real, c1 real, c2 real, R real, S real, na0 real, na1 real, na2 real, ca0 real, ca1 real, ca2 real, b0 real, b1 real, b2 real, hl int, l0 real, l1 real, l2 real, sg real, k real, ang real, fsid int)` is the second half of JavaScript's one, the deformation for each tool. In every loop below i runs from 0 below n in order, v is `QV`[i], a is 3v, w is read from `QW`[i] as a real, and "skip when w is falsy" means skip i when bad(w). Tool 0 or 1: w is `QW`[i]·k; skip when w is falsy; set(v, `CO`[a] plus na0·w, `CO`[a+1] plus na1·w, `CO`[a+2] plus na2·w). Tool 2: the same but with `VN`[a]·w, `VN`[a+1]·w, `VN`[a+2]·w added. Tool 3: bx is c0 minus (na0·R)·0.5, by is c1 minus (na1·R)·0.5, bz is c2 minus (na2·R)·0.5; w is `QW`[i]·k; skip when w is falsy; nrm(`CO`[a] minus bx, `CO`[a+1] minus by, `CO`[a+2] minus bz), copied to u0, u1, u2; set(v, `CO`[a] plus u0·w, `CO`[a+1] plus u1·w, `CO`[a+2] plus u2·w). Tool 4 or 5: cr is true when tool is 4; w is `QW`[i]; skip when w is falsy; px is c0 minus `CO`[a], py is c1 minus `CO`[a+1], pz is c2 minus `CO`[a+2]; pd is (px·na0 plus py·na1) plus pz·na2; f is jmin(1.0, (w·S)·0.5)·(0.5 when cr is true, else sg); h is (−w)·k when cr is true, else 0.0; set(v, (`CO`[a] plus (px minus na0·pd)·f) plus na0·h, (`CO`[a+1] plus (py minus na1·pd)·f) plus na1·h, (`CO`[a+2] plus (pz minus na2·pd)·f) plus na2·h). Tool 7, 8, 9, 10 or 11: off is R·0.25 for tool 7, R·0.35 for tool 8 and 0.0 otherwise; px is ca0 plus (na0·off)·sg, py is ca1 plus (na1·off)·sg, pz is ca2 plus (na2·off)·sg; side is −sg for tool 10, sg for tool 11, 0.0 for tool 9 and −sg otherwise; then w is `QW`[i]; skip when w is falsy; d is ((`CO`[a] minus px)·na0 plus (`CO`[a+1] minus py)·na1) plus (`CO`[a+2] minus pz)·na2; skip when side is not 0 and d·side is at most 0; f is jmin(1.0, (w·S)·0.5)·(sg for tool 9, else 1.0); set(v, `CO`[a] minus (na0·d)·f, `CO`[a+1] minus (na1·d)·f, `CO`[a+2] minus (na2·d)·f). Tool 12: h is ang divided by 2.0; cs is sc_cos(h) and sn is sc_sin(h)·sg; m0 is na0·cs plus b0·sn, m1 is na1·cs plus b1·sn, m2 is na2·cs plus b2·sn; g0 is na0·cs minus b0·sn, g1 is na1·cs minus b1·sn, g2 is na2·cs minus b2·sn; then w is `QW`[i]; skip when w is falsy; rx is `CO`[a] minus ca0, ry is `CO`[a+1] minus ca1, rz is `CO`[a+2] minus ca2; P0, P1, P2 are m0, m1, m2 when (rx·b0 plus ry·b1) plus rz·b2 is at least 0, else g0, g1, g2; d is (rx·P0 plus ry·P1) plus rz·P2; skip when d·sg is at most 0; f is jmin(1.0, (w·S)·0.5); set(v, `CO`[a] minus (P0·d)·f, `CO`[a+1] minus (P1·d)·f, `CO`[a+2] minus (P2·d)·f). Tool 13: `TMP` is emptied and then resized to 3n zeros; first loop: m is nbr(v); x, y, z start at 0.0; for j below m, with q equal to 3·`NB`[j], x grows by `CO`[q], y by `CO`[q+1] and z by `CO`[q+2]; f is jmin(1.0, w·S) when m is above 0, else 0.0; dv is m as a real, or 1.0 when m is 0; `TMP`[3i] becomes `CO`[a] plus (x divided by dv minus `CO`[a])·f, `TMP`[3i+1] becomes `CO`[a+1] plus (y divided by dv minus `CO`[a+1])·f, `TMP`[3i+2] becomes `CO`[a+2] plus (z divided by dv minus `CO`[a+2])·f; second loop: unless w is falsy, set(v, `TMP`[3i], `TMP`[3i+1], `TMP`[3i+2]). Tool 14: h is R·0.25; w is `QW`[i]; skip when w is falsy; L is jmax(−1.0, jmin(1.0, `LAY`[v] plus ((w·S)·0.3)·sg)); `LAY`[v] becomes L as f32; set(v, `ORIG`[a] plus (`ON`[a]·L)·h, `ORIG`[a+1] plus (`ON`[a+1]·L)·h, `ORIG`[a+2] plus (`ON`[a+2]·L)·h) using the unrounded L. Tool 15 when hl is 1: nrm(l0, l1, l2) copied to u0, u1, u2; then w is `QW`[i]·jmin(1.0, S); skip when w is falsy; px is c0 minus `CO`[a], py is c1 minus `CO`[a+1], pz is c2 minus `CO`[a+2]; pd is (px·u0 plus py·u1) plus pz·u2; set(v, (`CO`[a] plus l0·w) plus ((px minus u0·pd)·w)·0.3, (`CO`[a+1] plus l1·w) plus ((py minus u1·pd)·w)·0.3, (`CO`[a+2] plus l2·w) plus ((pz minus u2·pd)·w)·0.3). Tool 17 or 18 when hl is 1: e is (l0·na0 plus l1·na1) plus l2·na2; tx is l0 minus na0·e, ty is l1 minus na1·e, tz is l2 minus na2·e; then w is `QW`[i]·S; skip when w is falsy; d is (((`CO`[a] minus ca0)·na0 plus (`CO`[a+1] minus ca1)·na1) plus (`CO`[a+2] minus ca2)·na2)·jmin(1.0, w·0.3) for tool 18, else 0.0; set(v, (`CO`[a] plus tx·w) minus na0·d, (`CO`[a+1] plus ty·w) minus na1·d, (`CO`[a+2] plus tz·w) minus na2·d). Tool 19: w is jmin(1.0, `QW`[i]·S); skip when w is falsy; m is `MASK`[v]; `MASK`[v] becomes jmax(0.0, jmin(1.0, m plus (1.0 minus m when sg is above 0, else −m)·w)) as f32; maskV(v). Tool 20: `TMP` is emptied and then resized to n zeros; first loop: m is nbr(v); x starts at 0.0 and grows by `MASK`[`NB`[j]] for j below m; `TMP`[i] becomes `MASK`[v] plus (x divided by m minus `MASK`[v])·jmin(1.0, w·S) when m is above 0, else `MASK`[v]; second loop: `MASK`[v] becomes `TMP`[i] as f32 and maskV(v). Tool 21: skip i when `QD`[i] is above R·0.7; m is vtris(v); `WL` is emptied and then gets `TB`[0] to `TB`[m−1] in order; setFS(t, fsid) for each t of `WL` in order. Any other tool does nothing. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

`edgeOk(e int, a int, b int) bool` is true when `TDEAD`[e divided by 3, integer division] is 0, `TRI`[nx(e)] equals a and `TRI`[pv(e)] equals b. `triQ(a int, b int, d int) real` is JavaScript's triQ: A, B and D are 3a, 3b and 3d; u0, u1, u2 are `CO`[B] minus `CO`[A], `CO`[B+1] minus `CO`[A+1], `CO`[B+2] minus `CO`[A+2]; w0, w1, w2 are `CO`[D] minus `CO`[A], `CO`[D+1] minus `CO`[A+1], `CO`[D+2] minus `CO`[A+2]; x0, x1, x2 are `CO`[D] minus `CO`[B], `CO`[D+1] minus `CO`[B+1], `CO`[D+2] minus `CO`[B+2]; L is ((u0·u0 plus u1·u1) plus u2·u2 plus ((w0·w0 plus w1·w1) plus w2·w2)) plus ((x0·x0 plus x1·x1) plus x2·x2), summing the three dot products left to right; when L is above 0 it returns ((2.0·sqrt(3.0))·hyp3(u1·w2 minus u2·w1, u2·w0 minus u0·w2, u0·w1 minus u1·w0)) divided by L, else 0.0. `nn(x int, y int, z int)` sets `U0`, `U1`, `U2` to the cross product (q minus p) × (r minus p) where p, q and r are the points `CO`[3x..3x+2], `CO`[3y..3y+2] and `CO`[3z..3z+2]: with e0, e1, e2 equal to q minus p and f0, f1, f2 equal to r minus p per component, `U0` is e1·f2 minus e2·f1, `U1` is e2·f0 minus e0·f2 and `U2` is e0·f1 minus e1·f0.

`flipE(c int, qm bool) bool` is JavaScript's flipE. o is `OO`[c]; return false when o is below 0. c1 is nx(c), c2 is pv(c), o1 is nx(o), o2 is pv(o); a is `TRI`[c], b is `TRI`[c1], d is `TRI`[c2], e is `TRI`[o]. Return false when a equals e, or when `VLOCK`[b] and `VLOCK`[d] are both not 0. va is nbr(a); return false when any of `NB`[0] to `NB`[va−1] equals e. vb is nbr(b), vd is nbr(d) and ve is nbr(e) (only the counts are used); return false when vb is at most 3 or vd is at most 3. When qm is true: return false when jmin(triQ(a, b, e), triQ(e, d, a)) is at most 1.2·jmin(triQ(a, b, d), triQ(e, d, b)). When qm is false: tg(x) is 4 when `VLOCK`[x] is not 0, else 6; dev(n, x) is the absolute value of n minus tg(x); before is ((dev(va, a) plus dev(ve, e)) plus dev(vb, b)) plus dev(vd, d) and after is ((dev(va+1, a) plus dev(ve+1, e)) plus dev(vb−1, b)) plus dev(vd−1, d); return false when after is at least before. Then nn(a, b, d) gives n0; nn(e, d, b) gives n1; m is n0 plus n1 per component; nn(a, b, e) gives p0; nn(e, d, a) gives p1; lm is hyp3(m0, m1, m2); return false when lm is below 1e-30, or (p0·m, a dot product left to right) is at most (0.3·hyp3(p0))·lm, or (p1·m) is at most (0.3·hyp3(p1))·lm. Then X2 is `OO`[c1] and Y2 is `OO`[o1]; `TRI`[c2] becomes e; `TRI`[o2] becomes a; `OO`[c] becomes Y2, and when Y2 is at least 0, `OO`[Y2] becomes c; `OO`[o] becomes X2, and when X2 is at least 0, `OO`[X2] becomes o; `OO`[c1] becomes o1; `OO`[o1] becomes c1; `VC`[d] becomes o1; `VC`[b] becomes c1; chgT(c divided by 3); chgT(o divided by 3) (integer division); for v equal to a, b, d and e in that order, when `DS`[v] differs from `DQ`, `DS`[v] becomes `DQ` and v is pushed onto `MV`. Return true. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

`splitE(c int) int` is JavaScript's splitE. o is `OO`[c]; t0 is c divided by 3 and t1 is o divided by 3 when o is at least 0, else −1 (integer division); a0 is `TRI`[c], b is `TRI`[nx(c)], d is `TRI`[pv(c)]; m is addV(b); t2 is addT(t0); t3 is addT(t1) when t1 is at least 0, else −1. c1 is nx(c), c2 is pv(c), k0 is 3·t2, k1 is k0 plus 1, k2 is k0 plus 2. `TRI`[c2] becomes m, `TRI`[k0] becomes a0, `TRI`[k1] becomes m, `TRI`[k2] becomes d; x is `OO`[c1]; `OO`[k1] becomes x, and when x is at least 0, `OO`[x] becomes k1; `OO`[c1] becomes k2; `OO`[k2] becomes c1. When o is at least 0: o1 is nx(o), o2 is pv(o), e is `TRI`[o], j0 is 3·t3, j1 is j0 plus 1, j2 is j0 plus 2; `TRI`[o1] becomes m, `TRI`[j0] becomes e, `TRI`[j1] becomes d, `TRI`[j2] becomes m; y is `OO`[o2]; `OO`[j2] becomes y, and when y is at least 0, `OO`[y] becomes j2; `OO`[o2] becomes j1; `OO`[j1] becomes o2; `OO`[k0] becomes j0; `OO`[j0] becomes k0; chgT(t1). Otherwise `OO`[k0] becomes −1. Then `VC`[m] becomes c2, `VC`[d] becomes k2, chgT(t0); B is 3b and D is 3d; `MASK`[m] becomes ((`MASK`[b] plus `MASK`[d]) divided by 2.0) as f32; sc_vmid(m, b, d); set(m, (`CO`[B] plus `CO`[D]) divided by 2.0, (`CO`[B+1] plus `CO`[D+1]) divided by 2.0, (`CO`[B+2] plus `CO`[D+2]) divided by 2.0); ownV(m, `TLEAF`[t0]); colV(m); it returns m.

`collapseE(c int) bool` is JavaScript's collapseE. o is `OO`[c]; return false when o is below 0. t0 is c divided by 3 and t1 is o divided by 3 (integer division); a is `TRI`[c], b is `TRI`[nx(c)], d is `TRI`[pv(c)], e is `TRI`[o]. Return false when a equals e or any of `VLOCK`[b], `VLOCK`[d], `VH`[b], `VH`[d] is not 0. `RB` is emptied and gets `NB`[0] to `NB`[nbr(b)−1] in order; `RD` likewise from nbr(d); sh counts the entries x of `RB` (in order, duplicates counted each time) for which some entry of `RD` equals x; return false when sh differs from 2, or nbr(a) is at most 3, or nbr(e) is at most 3. X1 is `OO`[pv(c)], X2 is `OO`[nx(c)], Y1 is `OO`[pv(o)], Y2 is `OO`[nx(o)]; return false when any of them is below 0. B is 3b and D is 3d; P0, P1, P2 are (`CO`[B] plus `CO`[D]) divided by 2.0, (`CO`[B+1] plus `CO`[D+1]) divided by 2.0, (`CO`[B+2] plus `CO`[D+2]) divided by 2.0. `FBA` is emptied and gets `FB`[0] to `FB`[fan(b)−1]; `FDA` is emptied and gets `FB`[0] to `FB`[fan(d)−1]. The new position of a vertex x is P when x equals b or d, else its `CO` point. For each corner k of `FBA` in order and then of `FDA` in order: t is k divided by 3 (integer division); skip it when t equals t0 or t1; the corners of t have vertices A0, A1, A2 equal to `TRI`[3t], `TRI`[3t+1], `TRI`[3t+2]; with q0, q1, q2 their new positions and r0, r1, r2 their `CO` points, n1 is (q1 minus q0) × (q2 minus q0) and n0 is (r1 minus r0) × (r2 minus r0) (cross products written out as in nn); l1 is hyp3 of n1 and l0 is hyp3 of n0; return false when l1 is below 1e-30 or the dot product n0·n1 (left to right) is at most (0.2·l0)·l1. Then `OO`[X1] becomes X2, `OO`[X2] becomes X1, `OO`[Y1] becomes Y2, `OO`[Y2] becomes Y1. For each corner k of `FDA` in order: t is k divided by 3; when t differs from t0 and from t1, `TRI`[k] becomes b and chgT(t). `VC`[b] becomes nx(X1), `VC`[a] becomes pv(X1), `VC`[e] becomes nx(Y2). For t equal to t0 and then t1: `TDEAD`[t] becomes 1, rmT(t), chgT(t). `VDEAD`[d] becomes 1; rmV(d); `VC`[d] becomes −1; `VH`[d] becomes 1; `MASK`[b] becomes ((`MASK`[b] plus `MASK`[d]) divided by 2.0) as f32; sc_vmid(b, b, d); set(b, P0, P1, P2); colV(b); return true. Every local growable array in this paragraph is declared empty and then immediately gets `reserve`(its final length) before it is resized or appended to. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi.

`relax(c0 real, c1 real, c2 real, R real)` is JavaScript's relax with lam equal to 0.5. R2 is R·R. `WL` is emptied and then gets, in order, each v of `MV` for which `VDEAD`[v], `VLOCK`[v] and `VH`[v] are all 0 and ((`CO`[3v] minus c0)·(`CO`[3v] minus c0) plus (`CO`[3v+1] minus c1)·(`CO`[3v+1] minus c1)) plus (`CO`[3v+2] minus c2)·(`CO`[3v+2] minus c2) is at most R2, all tested before any vertex moves. For each v of `WL` in order: m is nbr(v); skip v when m is below 3; x, y, z start at 0.0 and for j below m, with q equal to 3·`NB`[j], x grows by `CO`[q], y by `CO`[q+1] and z by `CO`[q+2]; a is 3v; x becomes x divided by m minus `CO`[a], y becomes y divided by m minus `CO`[a+1], z becomes z divided by m minus `CO`[a+2]; dd is (x·`VN`[a] plus y·`VN`[a+1]) plus z·`VN`[a+2]; q0 is `CO`[a] plus (x minus `VN`[a]·dd)·0.5, q1 is `CO`[a+1] plus (y minus `VN`[a+1]·dd)·0.5, q2 is `CO`[a+2] plus (z minus `VN`[a+2]·dd)·0.5; k is vtris(v); ok starts true; for j below k while ok is true: t is `TB`[j]; with the corner points of t taken from `CO`, except that a corner whose vertex equals v uses (`CO`[a], `CO`[a+1], `CO`[a+2]) for n0 and (q0, q1, q2) for n1: n0 and n1 are (p1 minus p0) × (p2 minus p0) over the three corners in order (cross products written out); l0 is hyp3 of n0 and l1 is hyp3 of n1; ok becomes true only when l1 is above 1e-30, the dot product n0·n1 is above (0.5·l0)·l1, and (n1 0·`VN`[a] plus n1 1·`VN`[a+1]) plus n1 2·`VN`[a+2] is above 0.5·l1. When ok is still true, set(v, q0, q1, q2). Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

`cr(i int) real` reads the coordinate array that JavaScript's topo captured at its start: when `STL` is 0 it is `CO`[i]; when `STL` is 1 it is `SNP`[i] when i is below the length of `SNP`, else NaN. `elen(a int, b int) real` is hyp3(cr(3a) minus cr(3b), cr(3a+1) minus cr(3b+1), cr(3a+2) minus cr(3b+2)). `edges(t int)` is JavaScript's edges closure: for k below 3: e is 3t plus k; o is `OO`[e]; skip k when o is at least 0 and o is below e; a is `TRI`[nx(e)] and b is `TRI`[pv(e)]; push e, a and b onto `EE` and elen(a, b) onto `EL`. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

The exported function `sc_topo(c0 real, c1 real, c2 real, R real, L real, simp i32, budget i32) i32` is JavaScript's topo(c, R, L, simp, budget); it calls views() before every return. Return 0 when `DYN` is 0 or L is not above 0. n is range(c0, c1, c2, R); return 0 when n is 0. `TOP` becomes 1 and `STL` becomes 0. n0 is the length of `MV`; `QID` grows by 1 and s is `QID`; `EE` and `EL` are emptied; hi is L and lo is L·(0.8 when simp is not 0, else 0.4); R2 is (R·R)·1.44. inR(a, b) is true when, with x equal to (cr(3a) plus cr(3b)) divided by 2.0 minus c0, y equal to (cr(3a+1) plus cr(3b+1)) divided by 2.0 minus c1 and z equal to (cr(3a+2) plus cr(3b+2)) divided by 2.0 minus c2, (x·x plus y·y) plus z·z is at most R2. `VS` is emptied and gets `QV`[0] to `QV`[n−1]. For each v of `VS` in order: m is vtris(v); for j below m: t is `TB`[j]; when `TQ`[t] differs from s, `TQ`[t] becomes s and edges(t). ops is 0. Entry i of the edge list is (`EE`[3i], `EE`[3i+1], `EE`[3i+2]) with length `EL`[i]. When simp is 0: `QL` is emptied and gets every index i of the edge list whose length is above hi, in order, and is then stable-sorted by length descending (i goes before j only when `EL`[i] is above `EL`[j]); for pass from 0 while `QL` is not empty, pass is below 7 and ops is below budget: ne is the length of `EL`; for each index i of `QL` in order: stop this loop when ops is at least budget; with (e, a, b) the edge i: skip it unless edgeOk(e, a, b) is true, `OO`[e] is at least 0, elen(a, b) is above hi and inR(a, b) is true; m is splitE(e); ops grows by 1; k is vtris(m); `WL` is emptied and gets `TB`[0] to `TB`[k−1]; edges(t) for each t of `WL` in order. After the loop `QL` is emptied and gets every index i from ne below the length of `EL` whose length is above hi, in order, then stable-sorted by length descending as before. Then `QL` is emptied and gets every index i of the edge list whose length is below lo, in order, stable-sorted by length ascending (i goes before j only when `EL`[i] is below `EL`[j]); for each index i of `QL` in order: stop when ops is at least budget; with (e, a, b) the edge i: skip it unless edgeOk(e, a, b), elen(a, b) is below lo and inR(a, b); when collapseE(e) is true, ops grows by 1. Then for pass from 0 below 2: `QID` grows by 1 and s2 is `QID`; `FL` is emptied; tn is the length of `MV`; for i below tn: v is `MV`[i]; skip v when `VDEAD`[v] is not 0; m is vtris(v); top is true when i is at least n0; for j below m: t is `TB`[j]; skip t when `TQ`[t] equals s2; `TQ`[t] becomes s2; q is triQ(`TRI`[3t], `TRI`[3t+1], `TRI`[3t+2]); when q is below 0.4: bk is 0 and bl is −1.0; for kk below 3, l is elen(`TRI`[3t + (kk+1) mod 3], `TRI`[3t + (kk+2) mod 3]) and when l is above bl, bl becomes l and bk becomes kk; push 3t plus bk, `TRI`[3t + (bk+1) mod 3], `TRI`[3t + (bk+2) mod 3] and 1 onto `FL`. When top is true: for kk below 3: e is 3t plus kk; o is `OO`[e]; when o is above e, push e, `TRI`[nx(e)], `TRI`[pv(e)] and 0 onto `FL`. Then f is 0; for each entry (e, a, b, q) of `FL` (4 ints each) in order: when edgeOk(e, a, b) is true and flipE(e, q equal to 1) is true, f grows by 1. When f is 0 the pass loop stops. Then when ops is above 0, relax(c0, c1, c2, R). Then `TOP` and `STL` become 0, `SNP` is emptied, views(), and it returns ops as i32. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf.

`moveT(s int, d int)` is JavaScript's moveT: for k below 3: cs is 3s plus k and cd is 3d plus k; v is `TRI`[cs] and o is `OO`[cs]; `TRI`[cd] becomes v; `OO`[cd] becomes o; when o is at least 0, `OO`[o] becomes cd; when `VC`[v] equals cs, `VC`[v] becomes cd; `TN`[cd] becomes `TN`[cs]. Then `TF`[d] becomes `TF`[s], `TSET`[d] becomes `TSET`[s], `TH`[d] becomes `TH`[s], `TDEAD`[d] becomes 0, `TDEAD`[s] becomes 1; sc_fcp(d, s); lf is `TLEAF`[s] and i is `TPOS`[s]; when lf is at least 0, entry i of the triangle list of lf becomes d and `TPOS`[d] becomes i; `TLEAF`[d] becomes lf; `TLEAF`[s] becomes −1; chgT(d). `moveV(s int, d int)` is JavaScript's moveV: n is fan(s); for i below n: `TRI`[`FB`[i]] becomes d and chgT(`FB`[i] divided by 3); for k below 3: `CO`[3d+k] becomes `CO`[3s+k], `POS`[3d+k] becomes `POS`[3s+k], `VN`[3d+k] becomes `VN`[3s+k], `COL`[3d+k] becomes `COL`[3s+k]; `MASK`[d] becomes `MASK`[s], `VH`[d] becomes `VH`[s], `VLOCK`[d] becomes `VLOCK`[s], `VC`[d] becomes `VC`[s]; sc_vcp(d, s); lf is `VLEAF`[s]; when lf is at least 0: i is `VPOS`[s], entry i of the vertex list of lf becomes d and `VPOS`[d] becomes i; `VLEAF`[d] becomes lf; `VLEAF`[s] becomes −1; `VDEAD`[d] becomes 0; `VDEAD`[s] becomes 1; `VC`[s] becomes −1; chgV(d). The exported function `sc_compact()` is JavaScript's compact: when `DYN` is 1: for t from `NT` minus 1 down to 0, when `TDEAD`[t] is not 0: l is `NT` minus 1; when l differs from t, moveT(l, t); `TDEAD`[l] becomes 0; `NT` decreases by 1. Then for v from `NV` minus 1 down to 0, when `VDEAD`[v] is not 0: l is `NV` minus 1; when l differs from v, moveV(l, v); `VDEAD`[l] becomes 0; `NV` decreases by 1. Then views().

The exported function `sc_buildO()` is JavaScript's buildO. nt is `NT` and nv is `NV`. cnt is a local i32 list of nv plus 1 zeros; for c below 3nt, cnt[`TRI`[c] plus 1] grows by 1; for v below nv, cnt[v+1] grows by cnt[v]. L is a local i32 list of 3nt zeros and q a local copy of the first nv entries of cnt; for c below 3nt in order, L[q[`TRI`[c]]] becomes c and q[`TRI`[c]] grows by 1. `OO`[c] becomes −1 for c below 3nt. For each c below 3nt: u is `TRI`[nx(c)] and w is `TRI`[pv(c)]; hit is −1, k is 0 and s is 0; for i from cnt[w] below cnt[w+1]: dd is L[i]; when `TRI`[nx(dd)] equals u, hit becomes pv(dd) and k grows by 1; for i from cnt[u] below cnt[u+1], when `TRI`[nx(L[i])] equals w, s grows by 1; when k is 1 and s is 1, `OO`[c] becomes hit. vis is a local u8 list of 3nt zeros. For each v below nv: g is 0; for i from cnt[v] below cnt[v+1]: c0 is L[i]; skip it when vis[c0] is not 0; uu is v when g is 0, else addV(v); g grows by 1; `WL` is emptied; c is c0; open is false; repeat: vis[c] becomes 1, c is pushed onto `WL`, p is `OO`[pv(c)], c becomes −1 when p is below 0, else pv(p); and repeat again only while c is at least 0 and vis[c] is 0. When c is below 0: open becomes true; p is `OO`[nx(c0)]; c becomes −1 when p is below 0, else nx(p); while c is at least 0 and vis[c] is 0: vis[c] becomes 1, c is pushed onto `WL`, r is `OO`[nx(c)], c becomes −1 when r is below 0, else nx(r). For each corner k of `WL`, `TRI`[k] becomes uu; `VC`[uu] becomes c0; `VLOCK`[uu] becomes 1 when open is true, else 0. After the corners of v, when g is 0, `VLOCK`[v] becomes 1. `CAV` and `CAT` become 1. Then views(). Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf. Every local growable array in this paragraph is declared empty and then immediately gets `reserve`(its final length) before it is resized or appended to.

The exported function `sc_check() i32` is JavaScript's check writing its messages as triples (kind, x, y) onto `CHK`, which is emptied first; the message count is the length of `CHK` divided by 3. When `DYN` is 0 it returns 0. For c from 0 while c is below 3·`NT` and the count is below 20: t is c divided by 3; when `TDEAD`[t] is not 0, push (1, t, 0) and go to the next c. o is `OO`[c]; when o is at least 0 and (`OO`[o] differs from c, or `TRI`[nx(o)] differs from `TRI`[pv(c)], or `TRI`[pv(o)] differs from `TRI`[nx(c)]), push (2, c, 0). With x equal to `TRI`[c]: when x is below 0, or x is at least `NV`, or `VDEAD`[x] is not 0, push (3, c, 0). Then for v from 0 while v is below `NV` and the count is below 20: when `VDEAD`[v] is not 0, push (4, v, 0) and go to the next v; c is `VC`[v]; when c is at least 0 and `TRI`[c] differs from v, push (5, v, 0). Then lt is the sum of `LTN`[nd] over every nd below `NN` with `NL`[nd] below 0 and `LTC`[nd] at least 0; when lt differs from `NT`, push (7, lt, `NT`). Then views() and return the count as i32. Every push of an int onto a module i32 array in this paragraph is done with pi, and every push onto `EL` with pf. Every local growable array in this paragraph is declared empty and then immediately gets `reserve`(its final length) before it is resized or appended to.
